  "label": "Benchmarking Structure Based Binding Prediction",
  "related": [
    "conformational-protein-8241",
    "model-rna-906f",
    "builder-capsid-20a5"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [
    "ml-ai"
  ],
  "slug": "optimal-transport-flow-matching-ea35",
  "summary": "New benchmarks separate ligand placement from protein conformation, add apo/holo cross-state splits, and test pose ranking agents, redefining reported accuracy.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that benchmark construction and metric decomposition now determine what a structure-based predictor is credited with. A JCIM benchmark of AlphaFold3, RoseTTAFold3, Boltz-2 and Chai-1 on kinase and GPCR complexes finds correct ligand placement decoupled from accurate protein conformation, so aggregate success masks distinct failure modes. Pyrite turns pose selection into its own task with a 1,500-pair ranking benchmark, where LLM-based agents (PyriteReasoner, PyriteFeaturizer) reach up to 74% accuracy. AllositeUltra pairs cross-state representation learning with AsBench2, scoring allosteric site prediction on both apo and substrate-bound structures with prospective validation, while the K13 artemisinin-resistance classifiers lean on leave-one-out cross-validation over structure-derived physicochemical descriptors. Multimodal affinity and property models (DTBNet, MBFGNK) and generative or projection-based design methods (Pallatom2, PAMP, SF-GA) are differentiated less by architecture than by the evaluation regime and experimental checks reported.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-14"
}
